12
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:28,000
Uh

13
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,100
The goal is to utilize the existing computational resources

14
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,299
train a model that is significantly smarter than existing models

15
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:33,500
shock the world with China

16
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:35,420
So how exactly should we do it?

17
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:38,519
At that time, there were roughly two widely recognized laws about large models.

18
00:00:38,519 --> 00:00:40,419
The first law is called the model's skin law

19
00:00:40,419 --> 00:00:43,479
which means that for every order of magnitude increase in model scale

20
00:00:43,479 --> 00:00:45,340
the model may become slightly smarter

21
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:48,520
So the second, the second rule is that the computational requirements of the model

22
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,540
is roughly proportional to the number of model parameters

23
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:51,940
So this makes you think, right?

24
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:52,680
your intelligence

25
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,660
You need more parameters to make the model smarter

26
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:57,060
and more parameters mean more computational resources

27
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:59,060
Therefore, greater intelligence equals more computational resources.

28
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:01,200
So without purchasing more GPUs, we can't train smarter models

29
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,260
Oh, then we're stuck here, aren't we?

30
