31
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,560
What should we do if we want to train a smarter model?

32
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,780
So is there any way we can break through this dilemma?

33
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:12,750
Yes, the solution is to design sparse architectures

34
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:15,210
find ways to increase the model scale while

35
00:01:15,210 --> 00:01:16,850
without increasing the computational cost of the model

36
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,530
In that case

37
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:19,250
we can use the same number of GPUs

38
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,280
train a much larger model

39
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,840
Okay, so the next question is

40
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,830
How do we construct this sparsity?

41
00:01:25,830 --> 00:01:28,830
Let's start with a brief introduction to the basic architecture of large models

42
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:29,930
is the transformers architecture

43
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:32,290
This architecture consists of a series of Transformer modules

44
00:01:32,290 --> 00:01:33,910
stacked together like building blocks

45
00:01:33,910 --> 00:01:35,810
There are just two modules in each block

46
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:38,460
the attention module and the feed-forward network module

47
00:01:38,525 --> 00:01:40,900
The attention module is responsible for establishing

48
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,380
the interrelationships between words

49
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:45,370
while the feedforward network module performs in-depth understanding of the words themselves

50
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:46,530
For large models

51
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:47,710
feed-forward network module

52
00:01:47,710 --> 00:01:50,450
that is, the number of parameters for this deep understanding of the words themselves

53
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:52,169
accounts for about 80% of the entire model

54
00:01:52,169 --> 00:01:53,009
That is to say

55
00:01:53,009 --> 00:01:55,749
as long as we can make this feedforward network module ten times more efficient

56
00:01:55,749 --> 00:01:57,209
then with the same computational budget

57
00:01:57,209 --> 00:02:00,329
the number of parameter models can roughly double to eight times the original wow

58
00:02:00,329 --> 00:02:01,100
That's awesome

59
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:02,860
If we can use the same amount of model computational resources

60
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:04,060
train a model eight times larger

61
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:06,600
This is definitely going to shock the tech industry. Okay

62
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,320
So our goal is set

63
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:10,440
We want to sparsify the feedforward network modules

64
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:11,620
for example, sparsify it by tenfold

65
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,060
So how exactly do we do it?

66
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:15,010
The simplest approach is a two-step process

67
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:17,170
First, divide the parameters in the feedforward network module

68
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:19,170
evenly divide the parameters of each layer into ten parts

69
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:20,350
then create a selector

70
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:22,480
select one parameter from the ten partitions to execute

71
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:23,960
So if you also think this way

72
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:24,860
You're so smart

73
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:25,940
You've already designed it

74
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:29,330
The pioneer of mixture-of-experts models is called Switch Transformers

75
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,630
This is an extremely simplified version of the mixture of experts model.

76
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:35,110
Each set of parameters we mentioned earlier is called an expert.

77
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,830
So this approach of selecting one expert per layer

78
00:02:37,830 --> 00:02:39,270
Can it be further optimized?

79
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:40,560
Of course it can

80
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,960
The core flaw of this approach

81
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,960
As you might imagine, some relatively general capabilities

82
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:46,700
for example, understanding the semantics of a sentence

83
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,140
is a capability required to solve many problems

84
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:51,780
So if only one expert is working at each layer,

85
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:52,840
This means

86
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:53,860
regardless of the expert

87
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:56,420
more or less forced to learn these generic capabilities

88
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:57,420
What a waste that is!

89
00:02:57,420 --> 00:02:59,880
Different experts could have learned completely different things.

90
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:02,500
yet they end up being forced to learn these same generic capabilities

91
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,930
So we might as well do it this way

92
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:06,190
let some experts specialize in learning general capabilities

93
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:07,670
and then add some other experts

94
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:09,110
on the foundation of general capabilities

95
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:10,810
drawing strengths from various specialized domains

96
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:11,870
blossoming everywhere

97
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:12,950
Congratulations

98
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:15,970
You've just designed the two core technologies of Deep SG MoE

99
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:17,750
The first technique is the shared expert

100
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:19,790
which refers to the experts shared by all requests

101
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:21,470
We use it to learn general capabilities

102
00:03:21,470 --> 00:03:23,650
The second technique is fine-grained experts

103
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:25,130
make the granularity of experts finer

104
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:26,430
thereby expanding the number of experts

105
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:27,520
blossom everywhere

106
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:31,180
So the trainers happily used the technology provided by Deep SME

107
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:33,700
sparsified the feedforward network module by a factor of ten

108
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,940
happily ran the training script

109
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:38,279
So what happened as a result?

110
00:03:38,279 --> 00:03:40,039
Uh, the training suddenly crashed.

111
00:03:40,039 --> 00:03:41,579
Millions of yuan went up in smoke.

112
00:03:41,579 --> 00:03:42,639
promotions and salary increases are gone

113
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:43,839
or even lose their jobs

114
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:45,730
So why does the training crash?

115
00:03:45,730 --> 00:03:46,950
This is actually because

116
00:03:46,950 --> 00:03:49,530
The training process of the mixture of experts model is highly unstable.

117
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:51,850
Let's adopt the perspective of the expert selection module

118
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:53,480
Let's understand why this is the case

119
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,640
Let's say we are in a company

120
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:57,790
individual responsible for routing requests to experts

121
00:03:57,790 --> 00:03:58,870
Then you realize that

122
00:03:58,870 --> 00:04:01,450
Although all experts work at the same speed

123
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:04,470
but there are just a few experts whose capabilities grow very slowly

124
00:04:04,470 --> 00:04:07,440
then gradually the output quality of these experts will become increasingly lower

125
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:10,040
then naturally we will gradually

126
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:11,040
assign tasks to others

127
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:12,960
After all, if the final output efficiency is poor

128
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,180
we ourselves would also be held accountable

129
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:16,899
then ultimately these weak experts become weaker and weaker

130
00:04:16,899 --> 00:04:17,620
the less work they get

131
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:18,500
The less work they get, the weaker they become

132
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:19,899
eventually become the weakest experts

133
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:21,920
the overall intelligence of the entire model goes offline

134
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:24,600
In other words, the above process

135
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,100
is due to the training phase

136
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:28,160
the routing module aims to optimize model performance

137
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,370
while allocating all the training load to the stronger experts

138
00:04:30,370 --> 00:04:34,430
resulting in weaker experts being increasingly less likely to be selected by the routing module

139
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:36,070
it becomes increasingly difficult to receive training workload

140
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:37,290
eventually stops learning

141
00:04:37,290 --> 00:04:39,310
the overall intelligence of the model goes offline

142
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:41,490
So to solve this problem

143
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,390
The key is to design a mechanism

144
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:46,060
to balance the training load among different experts as evenly as possible

145
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:47,780
Well, this is actually a very deep topic

146
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:48,760
I won't go into detail

147
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,520
If you want to follow up on the research papers

148
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:53,140
STMOE and the lostfree balancing in the deep sic series of papers

149
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:56,490
free balancing is a great starting point

150
00:04:56,490 --> 00:04:59,410
So let's assume that the model trainers have gone through all the hardships

151
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:01,550
finally solved the model's stability issues

152
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:04,130
They happily handed the model over to the inference engineers

153
00:05:04,130 --> 00:05:05,210
and boasted

154
00:05:05,210 --> 00:05:05,710
Oh

155
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:08,210
My model can achieve the same performance with just 1/10 of the computational resources

156
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:10,250
achieve the same performance as the largest model

157
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:13,230
Then the model trainer got very excited

158
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:14,110
ran a test

159
00:05:14,110 --> 00:05:15,830
found it was completely not the case

160
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:17,310
This inference process is excruciatingly slow

161
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:18,250
not to mention ten times

162
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:19,630
twice the speed might even be a stretch

163
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:21,710
Why is the inference process so slow?

164
00:05:21,710 --> 00:05:25,210
This is because the mixture of experts model is less friendly to the inference process

165
00:05:25,210 --> 00:05:27,730
The reason is that during the training process

166
00:05:27,730 --> 00:05:30,370
a large number of inputs are dumped into the model all at once

167
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:32,780
basically all experts will be assigned tasks

168
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:35,860
so reading one expert's weights can serve many tokens

169
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:36,880
very cost-effective

170
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:38,530
However, during the training process

171
00:05:38,530 --> 00:05:40,090
especially during the decoding stage

172
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:43,250
each request generates only one new token at a time

173
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:46,490
Therefore, each request only activates a few experts per layer.

174
00:05:46,490 --> 00:05:48,290
This situation frequently occurs

175
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:50,130
I read all the weights of one expert

176
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:52,400
resulting in only serving one token in this scenario

177
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:55,100
This results in the model spending most of its inference time

178
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:56,980
is spent on reading expert weights

179
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:58,420
rather than on actual computation

180
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:00,289
So the efficiency naturally drops.

181
00:06:00,289 --> 00:06:01,569
To solve this problem

182
00:06:01,569 --> 00:06:03,329
We need to optimize the inference engine

183
00:06:03,329 --> 00:06:04,549
to improve inference performance

184
00:06:04,549 --> 00:06:07,060
These optimizations primarily serve three core purposes

185
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:09,120
The first objective is to improve operator efficiency

186
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:10,420
the core open-source libraries

187
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:11,120
for example, DB Station

188
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:14,789
DBEP and the GPU kernel written by perplexity

189
00:06:14,789 --> 00:06:17,830
The second core technology is the parallelization of communication and computation.

190
00:06:17,830 --> 00:06:20,490
This optimization leverages the underlying mechanism of the graphics card.

191
00:06:20,490 --> 00:06:23,530
As long as you can provide the graphics card with both communication load and computation load simultaneously

192
00:06:23,530 --> 00:06:26,530
then the GPU can simultaneously perform computation and communication without interference

193
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:29,550
The core technique of this method is primarily dual batch overlap

194
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:31,400
Dual batch overlap

195
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:32,520
Figuratively speaking,

196
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:35,120
is to have two sets of requests alternate like a ping-pong game

197
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,080
switching back and forth between the inference and computation processes of large models

198
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:39,940
thereby ensuring as much as possible that within the same time period

199
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:42,240
there is computational load from one set of requests

200
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,240
while communication load comes from another set of requests

201
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:45,360
In this way

202
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,440
we may we can achieve as much as possible

203
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:49,940
simultaneously utilizing the hardware's computational and communication capabilities

204
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:53,940
Third, try to enable large-scale parallel processing of the same model as much as possible

205
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:56,240
then distribute across multiple cards to serve more requests

206
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,760
This representative method is called large-scale expert parallelism

207
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:01,110
also known as white expert parallel y's batch

208
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:02,390
The core of this technique is

209
00:07:02,390 --> 00:07:05,270
to distribute the experts in the expert model across different GPUs

210
00:07:05,270 --> 00:07:07,750
this way the entire model occupies more GPUs

211
00:07:07,750 --> 00:07:09,180
can therefore handle higher loads

212
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:12,080
on average, the number of requests each expert receives increases

213
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:14,620
then reading the expert weights once can serve multiple requests

214
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:15,660
the efficiency is thus improved

215
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:18,040
At the same time, because the model is distributed across multiple GPUs

216
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,080
then the GPU memory usage of the model is reduced

217
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:22,100
more space is reserved for the kv cache

218
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:24,000
the inference batch size can be further increased

219
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,180
thereby further increasing the throughput

220
00:07:26,180 --> 00:07:28,700
So you see, starting from saving computational power in the beginning

221
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:29,920
to the intermediate load balancing

222
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,280
to the final race for speed

223
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:32,980
the evolution of Mixture of Experts models

224
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:34,460
is essentially within limited resources

225
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:36,500
especially under the constraints of limited GPU resources

226
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,160
model trainers and inference engineers rack their brains

227
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:40,260
outsmarting the graphics cards

228
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:42,409
the history of squeezing every last bit of computing power

229
00:07:42,729 --> 00:07:43,729
A very brilliant explanation

230
00:07:43,729 --> 00:07:46,069
Thank you for the bandwidth

231
00:07:46,069 --> 00:07:47,140
